        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;min-height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#ffffff;font-family:Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:3px solid #0f172a;border-bottom:1px solid #0f172a;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>LIBRARY · glossary</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:500 clamp(40px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.025em;text-wrap:balance;color:#0f172a'>Glossary</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:italic 400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.35 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#475569'>The words of the Twilite Zone, and how each is stored.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;flex-direction:row-reverse;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0f172a'>At a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #cbd5e1'><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Reading</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graph, node, edge, page, Portal</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Making</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>content, link, ground, faces, Glyph, template, workspace</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Building</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>root, Surface, Port, import, export, Bridge, class, Edge Class, Property, Method, invokes, Membrane, Specification, primitive</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Settled</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>2026-10-14</dd></dl></section></div><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Reading</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><span style='float:left;padding:6px 10px 0 0;font:500 68px/54px Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'>T</span>hese are the only words a visitor needs to read anything in the Twilite Zone.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Graph or node.</strong> The same thing is a graph or a node depending on where you stand. Inside it, it is a graph, with nodes of its own. From outside, it is a node: it has faces and a Glyph, and it sits in the workspace or behind a Portal like any other node. Use whichever word fits where the reader is standing.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>graph</strong> — A thing you can open: a set of nodes and the edges between them, saved as one <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>.node</code> file with its own address (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tlz://host/path/root.node</code>).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>node</strong> — One thing inside a graph. Stored as an entry in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>nodes</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>edge</strong> — A named connection between two nodes. Its name says what the connection means; click the name to read about that kind of connection. Stored as an entry in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>edges</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>page</strong> — What a graph or a node looks like when you are close enough to read it: its first, largest face.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Portal</strong> — A door to another graph. Click it to go through. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>portal</code>.</li></ul></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Making</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Words that arrive when someone starts making things.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>content</strong> — The words, pictures or markup a node shows. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>content</code>, with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.content.kind</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.content.value</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>link</strong> — An edge on the web: a connection to a graph or a web page, written in content or set on a node (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.link</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>url</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>href</code>). It is drawn as an edge, by the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>link</code> edge class, wherever both of its ends are in the workspace.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>ground</strong> — What you stand on inside a graph: where you land when you go in. A graph with graph content has one; a document has none. Joined to the root by a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ground</code> edge from the root&#39;s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ground</code> port.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>faces</strong> — How a graph or node looks from further away, largest to smallest. The author names them; the usual names are Detail, Summary and Icon. The first face is the page. What the app shows depends on a face&#39;s place in the order and the room it has, never on its name. The order ends at the Glyph. Stored as <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>face</code> nodes joined by <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>next-face</code> edges.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Glyph</strong> — A node&#39;s mark: the symbol that stands for it. Faces are how a node looks; the Glyph is how it is recognised. A graph has one because, seen from outside, a graph is a node. It is shown when there is no room for even the smallest face, and wherever the node is named in a small space: lists, the trail, link chips, tabs, bookmarks. It has its own editor and its name is always Glyph. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>glyph</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>template</strong> — A graph you start a new graph from. Templates are how the shapes that work get passed on, and each generation built from one can become a template in turn.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>workspace</strong> — Where open graphs sit side by side. Not stored in graphs.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Patterns, not kinds of graph</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A graph can grow into anything. Some shapes come up again and again: a page on its own, a picture, a list of doors to other graphs. People call these <strong>documents</strong>, <strong>scenes</strong> and <strong>collections</strong>. They are patterns, not kinds of graph: nothing in a file says which one a graph is, and a graph can be more than one at once. What a graph does follows from what is in it, never from a label on it (a graph with no ground shows its page because it has no ground). Templates are how the patterns are passed on.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Building</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Words for the template builder. A visitor never needs them.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>root</strong> (noun) — The node a graph begins from. It says what the graph is (its name, description and faces) and everything else in the graph hangs from it. Every graph has exactly one. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>root</code>, in the file <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>root.node</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>root</strong> (verb) — To select several graphs in the workspace and make a new graph from them, with a root of its own. It is what Twilite has instead of grouping. It works on graphs only: lifting nodes out of a graph into a new one is copy and paste. *Not built yet.*</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Surface</strong> — One ordered set of faces a graph shows from outside. A graph can have more than one. A <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>surface</code> edge from the root to the first face.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Port</strong> — A named place where a graph offers something to other graphs. The root is the graph&#39;s root Port. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>port</code>; an edge&#39;s ends are its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>sourcePort</code> and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>targetPort</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>export</strong> — What a graph offers to other graphs: a class, a set of classes, or the whole graph. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>export</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>import</strong> — What a graph uses from another graph&#39;s export. A class used in a graph must come in through an import. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Bridge</strong> — The connection from an export to the import that uses it: one graph drawing on another. A classed edge (the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>bridge</code> edge class), drawn in the workspace between the two graphs, and what Tidy arranges by. Not stored: worked out from the imports and exports of open graphs.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>class</strong> — The definition a node or edge follows: which Properties it has and how it looks. On nodes and edges alike, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>type</code> is the class key and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>classRef</code> the class graph&#39;s address.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Edge Class</strong> — A class for edges: what a kind of connection means and what it may join. A graph built from the Edge Class template.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Property</strong> — One named value on a node, as its class defines it. Every Property announces <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>property.changed</code> when its value changes; nothing happens unless an edge is drawn from it. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>property</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Method</strong> — The only code in a graph. It runs when something invokes it, and declares what it may change; the app refuses anything else. A Method has an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code> port of its own, so one Method can run the next. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>method</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>invokes</strong> — The edge that makes something happen: from what raises an event (a Property, a node, a button, a Method) to the Method it runs, naming the event. Every source draws its own edge: structure matters. Edge type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Membrane</strong> — A graph&#39;s boundary: what it holds in, and where its ports sit for things to cross. A graph grows from its root and is held by its membrane. Node type <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>membrane</code> for its control points, edge <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>membrane</code>, and the root&#39;s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.membrane</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Specification</strong> — The page that defines a primitive. An ordinary graph under <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>library/primitives/</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>primitive</strong> — One of the sixteen parts every graph is made of. Each has a Specification.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>In the app</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Words for finding your way.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>inside</strong> — Where you are after entering a graph: standing on its ground.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>trail</strong> — The path at the top of the window: Workspace › graph › node › Inside.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>going through</strong> — Following a Portal, or the end of a line, to where it leads.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Tidy</strong> — Arranging the workspace: graphs joined by a Bridge close together (imports on the left, exports on the right), Portal graphs further out.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Retired words</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Words older graphs and pages use, and what replaced them. A current graph never uses them.</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Declaration</strong>, <strong>manifest</strong> — the root.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>View</strong> — a face. <strong>View sequence</strong> — next-face.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>landing surface</strong> — the ground.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Field</strong> — a Property.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Markdown node</strong> — Content with kind <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>markdown</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Bridge node</strong> — an import or an export. The Bridge is the connection between them.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Graph Reference</strong> — a Portal.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>groups</strong> — rooting.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>frame</strong>, <strong>border</strong>, <strong>wrap</strong> (for a graph&#39;s boundary) — the Membrane.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>handle</strong> (in a file) — a Port. &quot;Handle&quot; is only the spot you grab on screen, and is never saved.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>Event Handler</strong>, <strong>Script</strong> — leaving in favour of Methods and the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>invokes</code> edge.</li></ul></section></div></div></div>"
        },
        "memo": "",
        "link": ""
      }
    },
