    {
      "id": "glossary-reading",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Reading",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Reading",
        "body": "These are the only words a visitor needs to read anything in the Twilite Zone.\n\n**Graph or node.** The same thing is a graph or a node depending on where you stand. Inside it, it is a graph, with nodes of its own. From outside, it is a node: it has faces and a Glyph, and it sits in the workspace or behind a Portal like any other node. Use whichever word fits where the reader is standing.\n\n- **graph** — A thing you can open: a set of nodes and the edges between them, saved as one `.node` file with its own address (`tlz://host/path/root.node`).\n- **node** — One thing inside a graph. Stored as an entry in `nodes`.\n- **edge** — A named connection between two nodes. Its name says what the connection means; click the name to read about that kind of connection. Stored as an entry in `edges`.\n- **page** — What a graph or a node looks like when you are close enough to read it: its first, largest face.\n- **Portal** — A door to another graph. Click it to go through. Node type `portal`.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592074867,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "side": "top",
          "offset": 0.2,
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": false,
      "resizable": true,
      "portPosition": "center",
      "showLabel": false
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-making",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Making",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Making",
        "body": "Words that arrive when someone starts making things.\n\n- **content** — The words, pictures or markup a node shows. Node type `content`, with `data.content.kind` and `data.content.value`.\n- **link** — An edge on the web: a connection to a graph or a web page, written in content or set on a node (`data.link`, `url`, `href`). It is drawn as an edge, by the `link` edge class, wherever both of its ends are in the workspace.\n- **ground** — What you stand on inside a graph: where you land when you go in. A graph with graph content has one; a document has none. Joined to the root by a `ground` edge from the root's `ground` port.\n- **faces** — How a graph or node looks from further away, largest to smallest. The author names them; the usual names are Detail, Summary and Icon. The first face is the page. What the app shows depends on a face's place in the order and the room it has, never on its name. The order ends at the Glyph. Stored as `face` nodes joined by `next-face` edges.\n- **Glyph** — A node's mark: the symbol that stands for it. Faces are how a node looks; the Glyph is how it is recognised. A graph has one because, seen from outside, a graph is a node. It is shown when there is no room for even the smallest face, and wherever the node is named in a small space: lists, the trail, link chips, tabs, bookmarks. It has its own editor and its name is always Glyph. Node type `glyph`.\n- **template** — A graph you start a new graph from. Templates are how the shapes that work get passed on, and each generation built from one can become a template in turn.\n- **workspace** — Where open graphs sit side by side. Not stored in graphs.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592074867,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "side": "top",
          "offset": 0.2,
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": false,
      "resizable": true,
      "portPosition": "center",
      "showLabel": false
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-patterns",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Patterns, not kinds of graph",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -280
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Patterns, not kinds of graph",
        "body": "A graph can grow into anything. Some shapes come up again and again: a page on its own, a picture, a list of doors to other graphs. People call these **documents**, **scenes** and **collections**. They are patterns, not kinds of graph: nothing in a file says which one a graph is, and a graph can be more than one at once. What a graph does follows from what is in it, never from a label on it (a graph with no ground shows its page because it has no ground). Templates are how the patterns are passed on.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592074867,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "side": "top",
          "offset": 0.2,
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": false,
      "resizable": true,
      "portPosition": "center",
      "showLabel": false
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-building",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Building",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": -280
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Building",
        "body": "Words for the template builder. A visitor never needs them.\n\n- **root** (noun) — The node a graph begins from. It says what the graph is (its name, description and faces) and everything else in the graph hangs from it. Every graph has exactly one. Node type `root`, in the file `root.node`.\n- **root** (verb) — To select several graphs in the workspace and make a new graph from them, with a root of its own. It is what Twilite has instead of grouping. It works on graphs only: lifting nodes out of a graph into a new one is copy and paste. *Not built yet.*\n- **Surface** — One ordered set of faces a graph shows from outside. A graph can have more than one. A `surface` edge from the root to the first face.\n- **Port** — A named place where a graph offers something to other graphs. The root is the graph's root Port. Node type `port`; an edge's ends are its `sourcePort` and `targetPort`.\n- **export** — What a graph offers to other graphs: a class, a set of classes, or the whole graph. Node type `export`.\n- **import** — What a graph uses from another graph's export. A class used in a graph must come in through an import. Node type `import`.\n- **Bridge** — The connection from an export to the import that uses it: one graph drawing on another. A classed edge (the `bridge` edge class), drawn in the workspace between the two graphs, and what Tidy arranges by. Not stored: worked out from the imports and exports of open graphs.\n- **class** — The definition a node or edge follows: which Properties it has and how it looks. On nodes and edges alike, `type` is the class key and `classRef` the class graph's address.\n- **Edge Class** — A class for edges: what a kind of connection means and what it may join. A graph built from the Edge Class template.\n- **Property** — One named value on a node, as its class defines it. Every Property announces `property.changed` when its value changes; nothing happens unless an edge is drawn from it. Node type `property`.\n- **Method** — The only code in a graph. It runs when something invokes it, and declares what it may change; the app refuses anything else. A Method has an `invokes` port of its own, so one Method can run the next. Node type `method`.\n- **invokes** — The edge that makes something happen: from what raises an event (a Property, a node, a button, a Method) to the Method it runs, naming the event. Every source draws its own edge: structure matters. Edge type `invokes`.\n- **Membrane** — A graph's boundary: what it holds in, and where its ports sit for things to cross. A graph grows from its root and is held by its membrane. Node type `membrane` for its control points, edge `membrane`, and the root's `data.membrane`.\n- **Specification** — The page that defines a primitive. An ordinary graph under `library/primitives/`.\n- **primitive** — One of the sixteen parts every graph is made of. Each has a Specification.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
